lets to move the race toward Democrats", "Prediction markets still price Pillen near ninety percent and most raters keep the race Likely or Solid R"], "rationale": "Polling has tightened to a four-to-five point race in the final month, and rating outlets have shifted several Plains races toward Democrats, which justifies shading the expected margin slightly closer than the ratings imply. Still, Pillen's incumbency, dominant fundraising, and Nebraska's strong Republican lean make him clearly favored.", "watch": "Whether independent polling in the final weeks confirms the tightening or Pillen's spending advantage and incumbency reassert a wider lead.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Jim Pillen (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Nebraska's governor's race has tightened in the closing weeks. Recent polls show Democratic nominee Lynne Walz within a few points of incumbent Jim Pillen, including one large October survey with the two statistically tied, and rating outlets have moved several Great Plains races toward Democrats. Even so, Pillen retains the structural advantages of incumbency in a state that leaned roughly ten points Republican at the presidential level.\n\nMoney tells a one-sided story: Pillen has raised roughly triple Walz's contributions and holds endorsements such as the Nebraska Farm Bureau. A no-bid contract probe and his decision to skip debates have given Walz openings, and she has sought cross-party support. Rating outlets remain split between Likely Republican and Solid Republican, and prediction markets price Pillen near ninety percent to win. The race is competitive but still tilts clearly toward the incumbent.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0219, "margin": -11.34}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0357, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0267, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0322, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0312, "margin": -11.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_